        "role": "original_answer",
        "label": "The answer, as recorded",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "p",
            "segments": [
              {
                "text": "If your landlord kept your entire security deposit, you have options. Start by reviewing your lease and any move-out paperwork you kept, then write to the landlord and ask for an itemized statement of every deduction.",
                "mark": null,
                "mode": null
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "segments": [
              {
                "text": "Most states give a landlord a set number of days after move-out to return the deposit or send that itemization. ",
                "mark": null,
                "mode": null
              },
              {
                "text": "The window usually falls somewhere between 14 and 45 days.",
                "mark": 1,
                "mode": "QUOTED_SPAN"
              },
              {
                "text": " ",
                "mark": null,
                "mode": null
              },
              {
                "text": "Once it has passed without an itemization, your position is much stronger.",
                "mark": 2,
                "mode": "QUOTED_SPAN"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "segments": [
              {
                "text": "Normal wear and tear is not a deductible item in most places. Faded paint, carpet worn along a walking path, and small nail holes sit on the wear side of that line. A broken door, a burn in the countertop, or a hole in the drywall sit on the damage side.",
                "mark": 4,
                "mode": "PASSAGE_CONTEXT"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "segments": [
              {
                "text": "I'm not able to tell you what your particular state requires, because deposit rules vary a great deal and I don't know where you live.",
                "mark": 3,
                "mode": "QUOTED_SPAN"
              },
              {
                "text": " Your best move is to look up your state's landlord-tenant statute or talk to a local attorney.",
                "mark": null,
                "mode": null
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "segments": [
              {
                "text": "If the landlord still will not pay after a written demand, small claims court is the usual next step. The filing fee is modest and you generally do not need a lawyer to appear. ",
                "mark": null,
                "mode": null
              },
              {
                "text": "Whether that is worth your time is a judgment only you can make.",
                "mark": 5,
                "mode": "QUOTED_SPAN"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "p",
            "segments": [
              {
                "text": "Keep every message in writing. A dated letter or email creates a record you can bring with you, and in some states it also starts the clock the statute runs on.",
                "mark": 6,
                "mode": "PASSAGE_CONTEXT"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
